// The first intent source, in a module of its own.
//
// Apart from the composition next door because a `.clk` module the Rust
// generator reads may not also declare a `udp_socket` -- the generator refuses
// one by name -- and `robot.clk` is where the seam's six sockets are. So this
// file is the cog and nothing else, and that file is the box, the sockets and
// the process.
//
// Scaffolding: when the intent bridge lands, this module, its host, `WakeParams`
// and `wake_state.clk` go with it, and the machine's scripts come from whatever
// asked for them. TODO(reachy-pod-motion-integration)
//
// It declares no signal group, and that is forced rather than a choice about
// what is worth counting: a module that imports two cog modules each importing
// the standard signal vocabulary is refused by the compiler for a conflicting
// serialization of a type both brought in, and `robot.clk` imports this module
// beside `motion`. So the two totals live in the state slot alone, where a
// reader of the slot finds them.
//
// Two modules are imported under aliases because a cog member below carries a
// name of its own: the output is called `script`, and the state slot's own
// module is `wake_state`, which the box that hosts this cog names a member for.
// A member name shadows a module name in the enclosing scope, which the
// compiler refuses as a redefinition.
// (A comment may not sit directly above a `use`, which is why this one is
// here.)

#![generate(cpp, cpp_cog, cpp_test_cog)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::cogs)]

use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] cogs::config;
use cogs::script as script_vocab;
use cogs::wake_state as wake_state_vocab;
use motion::timeline;

// The first intent source: one script, once, when the machine says it is
// commissioned.
//
// The session engages only on a script accepted in `resting`, and nothing on a
// machine publishes one -- the intent bridge is somebody else's work. So this
// box carries a minimal closed-loop source of its own: it watches the session's
// own narration for the transition into `resting`, publishes the configured
// gesture, and is finished.
//
// Woken by the reports rather than by a timer. Per-message waking keeps the gap
// between commissioning and the greeting at one scheduling hop -- which is the
// window `lead_ms` is sized against -- and means a view of eight over a
// thirty-two deep channel cannot be lapped between one publication and the read
// that follows it.
//
// Scaffolding: when the intent bridge lands, this cog and its configuration go
// with it. TODO(reachy-pod-motion-integration)
//
// The `simulation_options` at the foot say how long an execution is modelled to
// take. Nothing simulates this cog: its own input is the session's narration,
// published in the same box, but the box it lives in is composed over live
// sockets and carries no scenario target of its own. The figure is stated
// rather than defaulted anyway, so a value read anywhere has a statement behind
// it. (Neither a block header nor a field inside one may carry
// a comment, which is why this is here.)
cog Wake
{
    configs
    {
        // The lead the script is dated by, and the gesture itself.
        params: Tappy<config::WakeParams>;
    }

    states
    {
        // Whether the one script has gone out. Named for what it holds rather
        // than for the cog: a state member whose name matches its cog's
        // generates a C++ class nested inside one of the same name, which does
        // not compile.
        sent: Tappy<wake_state_vocab::WakeState>
        {
            mutable: true;
        }
    }

    inputs
    {
        // The session's narration. Sized to see every report rather than the
        // latest one: the transition this cog waits for is one row among a
        // burst of them, and a row superseded before it was read is a
        // commissioning nobody heard about.
        reports: Tappy<timeline::TimelineEntry>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }
    }

    outputs
    {
        script: Tappy<script_vocab::Script>;
    }

    execution
    {
        // Every report, no skips: the bound equals the view's size, so the
        // count is the window's length and never a clamp of it.
        condition got: new_message(reports, max=8);

        execute when: got;
    }

    simulation_options
    {
        execution_duration: 1ms;
    }
}
